A function-pointer member is a pointer wide, not a return value wide.

build_member parses the member's type name, then REPLACES the type when the
declarator turns out to be a function pointer:

    struct type* member_type = type_name();     /* `int` -- 4 bytes now */
    i->type = member_type;
    if(global_token->s[0] == '(')
    {
        i->name = parse_function_pointer();
        i->type = function_pointer;             /* 8 bytes, unsigned */
    }

and then, eighty lines further down, takes the SIZE and the SIGNEDNESS from
`member_type` -- the return type -- rather than from the type it just
assigned. For `int (*add)(int, int)` that is four bytes, signed.

create_struct reads i->size for BOTH the running offset and the alignment, so
the members after it overlap:

    struct Ops { int (*add)(int,int); int (*mul)(int,int); int tag; };
        micro-c   add@0  mul@4  tag@8    size 12
        gcc       add@0  mul@8  tag@16   size 24

The STORE is eight bytes wide, because that width comes from i->type, which is
correct. So `o.mul = do_mul` writes eight bytes at offset 4 and overwrites the
top half of `o.add`, and the first call through `o.add` jumps to an address
spliced from two different function pointers. SIGSEGV, which is what case 14
had been reporting.

Invisible until now for the usual reason: while `int` was eight bytes the
return type and the pointer were the same number. EXPERIMENT-zzw made them
differ. `void (*f)()` is still fine either way, which is why tcc's own
error_func at tcc.h:856 -- the only inline function-pointer member in the whole
tcc tree -- never showed this.

BOTH FIELDS, AND THE ARRAY BRANCH TOO. is_signed comes from the same wrong
place, so a function-pointer member was marked signed; at eight bytes that
never reached the emitted code, exactly as EXPERIMENT-zzzb's note says of the
bug it fixed in this same function. And the array branch computes
`count * member_type->size`, so an array of function pointers would get
four-byte elements. Sourcing all three from i->type fixes them together and
adds no new knowledge of what a function pointer's width is -- for every other
member i->type IS member_type, so the change is a no-op there.

THIS FIXES CASE 14 AND NOTHING ELSE. It is NOT the cause of the failing
programs in stage3-hermetic-arm64: 11-function-pointer-member is the same
twelve lines of C as case 14, but it is compiled BY mc-tcc, so tcc lays that
struct out, not micro-c. The resemblance is a trap and it cost a wrong
conclusion here before the tcc tree was checked. See EXPERIMENT-zzzc for the
fault that actually moved those four.

difftest aarch64 84/1 -> 85/0 with zzzc; case 14 is the one that closes.
--- a/cc_types.c
+++ b/cc_types.c
@@ -732,7 +732,7 @@
 		 * It also records that this member IS an array, which member access
 		 * needs: an array decays to its address and must not be loaded. */
 		i->is_array = TRUE;
-		i->size = constant_expression() * member_type->size;
+		i->size = constant_expression() * i->type->size;
 		if(0 == i->size)
 		{
 			fputs("Struct only supports [num] form\n", stderr);
@@ -742,7 +742,26 @@
 	}
 	else
 	{
-		i->size = member_type->size;
+		/* THE DECLARATOR'S OWN TYPE, NOT THE TYPE NAME IT STARTED FROM.
+		 *
+		 * For `int (*add)(int, int);` the branch above already replaced
+		 * i->type with function_pointer -- and then this line took the size
+		 * from `int`, the RETURN type, giving a member four bytes wide where
+		 * the pointer is eight. create_struct reads that for both the running
+		 * offset and the alignment, so the members after it overlap:
+		 *
+		 *     struct Ops { int (*add)(); int (*mul)(); int tag; }
+		 *     add@0 mul@4 tag@8            add and mul share four bytes
+		 *
+		 * The store is eight bytes wide, because THAT width comes from
+		 * i->type, so `o.mul = do_mul` overwrites the top half of `o.add` and
+		 * the first call through it jumps to a spliced address.
+		 *
+		 * Invisible while `int` was eight bytes: the return type and the
+		 * pointer were the same number. EXPERIMENT-zzw made them differ.
+		 * For every other member i->type IS member_type, so this is a no-op
+		 * there. */
+		i->size = i->type->size;
 	}
 
 	/* AND ITS SIGNEDNESS, which nothing here copied.
@@ -770,7 +789,7 @@
 	 *
 	 * An ARRAY member keeps the element's signedness, which is what a read of
 	 * one element needs. A function pointer has none to carry. */
-	i->is_signed = member_type->is_signed;
+	i->is_signed = i->type->is_signed;
 
 	member_size = i->size;
 
